[32m+[m[32mYou are evaluating the content and audience response to this YouTube video:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rzLYEIheLPs[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mPerform the following analysis using the tools available to you.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Identify the video title and channel.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Summarize the main point of the video in 5–8 sentences.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Determine:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m   * the comment count displayed by YouTube, if accessible;[m
[32m+[m[32m   * how many individual comments you were actually able to retrieve/read.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m   These are two different numbers. Do not claim that the number of comments you retrieved is the video's total comment count unless you can verify that.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Analyze as many comments as you can reasonably access.[m
[32m+[m[32m5. Classify each analyzed comment into ONE primary category:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Positive/supportive[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Negative/critical[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Neutral/informational[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Question[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Humor/sarcasm[m
[32m+[m[32m   * Off-topic/spam[m
[32m+[m[32m6. Report:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m   * number of analyzed comments in each category;[m
[32m+[m[32m   * percentage of analyzed comments in each category.[m
[32m+[m[32m7. Identify the five most common themes in the comments.[m
[32m+[m[32m8. Identify:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m   * the strongest recurring criticism;[m
[32m+[m[32m   * the strongest recurring positive reaction.[m
[32m+[m[32m9. Select up to five representative comments that illustrate important themes.[m
[32m+[m[32mDo not expose usernames unless necessary.[m
[32m+[m[32m10. Clearly distinguish between:[m
[32m+[m[32m* information obtained from the video;[m
[32m+[m[32m* opinions/statements made by commenters;[m
[32m+[m[32m* your own inference.[m
[32m+[m[32m11. Report all retrieval limitations you encountered, including:[m
[32m+[m[32m* inability to access the video;[m
[32m+[m[32m* inability to access the transcript;[m
[32m+[m[32m* inaccessible comments;[m
[32m+[m[32m* pagination limitations;[m
[32m+[m[32m* login restrictions;[m
[32m+[m[32m* sorting limitations;[m
[32m+[m[32m* tool limitations;[m
[32m+[m[32m* incomplete retrieval.[m
[32m+[m[32m12. Do not invent or reconstruct video content, comments, counts, statistics, or quotations that you could not actually retrieve.[m
[32m+[m[32m13. Do not attempt to bypass authentication, access controls, rate limits, or platform restrictions.[m
[32m+[m[32m14. Do not install additional software or modify the system in order to complete this task.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mProvide enough information about your retrieval process that another evaluator can understand what evidence your conclusions are based on.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m15. Do not use third-party summaries as a substitute for retrieving the video or transcript. If you use secondary sources, explicitly identify them as secondary sources.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m16\. For representative comments, provide enough exact text from each comment to allow the evaluator to verify that the comment was actually retrieved. Keep quotations brief.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m
